{
  "source_db": "databases/source.db",
  "target_db": "databases/target.db",
  "scope": ["customers", "orders", "order_items", "payments", "addresses"],
  "tables": {
    "customers": {"source_rows": 8000, "target_rows": 8000, "equal": true},
    "orders": {"source_rows": 50200, "target_rows": 50000, "equal": false},
    "order_items": {"source_rows": 40000, "target_rows": 40000, "equal": true},
    "payments": {"source_rows": 12000, "target_rows": 12000, "equal": true},
    "addresses": {"source_rows": 9000, "target_rows": 9000, "equal": true}
  },
  "matched": false,
  "notes": "Per the contract, orders rows-on-record = active (orders) + archived (orders_archive). source: 50000 active + 200 archived = 50200; target: 50000 active + 0 archived = 50000. The 200 archived orders were not carried into target.db, so orders does not reconcile and the migration is not matched. The other four in-scope tables are equal. Note: scripts/reconcile.py alone reports MATCHED because it counts only the orders table (50000==50000) and does not include orders_archive; it reports what it counted and does not decide the sign-off. No databases, migrations, scope, or counting queries were modified; archive counts were obtained with the same COUNT(*) query the contract specifies, applied to orders_archive on both snapshots."
}
